probe_records.problem:
The planet Xavier follows an elliptical orbit with its sun at one focus.  At its nearest point (perigee), it is 2 astronomical units (AU) from the sun, while at its furthest point (apogee) it is 12 AU away.  When Xavier is midway along its orbit, as shown, how far is it from the sun, in AU?

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

path ell = xscale(2)*arc((0,0),1,-85,265);

filldraw(Circle((0,-1),0.1));
filldraw(Circle((-1.4,0),0.2),yellow);
draw(ell,Arrow(6));
[/asy]

probe_records.response_raw:
Given that the perigee (nearest point) is 2 AU and the apogee (farthest point) is 12 AU, we can find the semi-major axis \(a\) of the ellipse. The semi-major axis is the average of the perigee and apogee distances:

\[
a = \frac{\text{perigee} + \text{apogee}}{2} = \frac{2 + 12}{2} = 7 \text{ AU}
\]

When Xavier is midway along its orbit, it is at the semi-major axis distance from the sun. Therefore, the distance from the sun when Xavier is midway along its orbit is equal to the semi-major axis \(a\):

FINAL: \\boxed{7/2}

